The main missing piece is **the relationship between an account and its owner**. `accountUser` should hold the user’s UUID, with a foreign key referencing `users.id`. That prevents accounts from referencing nonexistent users. [MariaDB foreign keys](https://mariadb.com/docs/server/architecture/server-constraints/foreign-key-constraints)

Your account definition could be:

```sql
CREATE TABLE scammistanbank.account (
    id VARCHAR(36) PRIMARY KEY,
    userId VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,
    balance DECIMAL(15, 2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 10000.00,
    openedAt TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,

    CONSTRAINT fk_account_user
        FOREIGN KEY (userId)
        REFERENCES scammistanbank.users(id)
        ON DELETE RESTRICT
) ENGINE=InnoDB;
```

This allows **one user to have multiple accounts**. Add `UNIQUE (userId)` only if each user should have exactly one account at most.

I checked your code, and these changes are still missing:

- **Drizzle schema:** remove `users.balance` and define the `account` table with its foreign key.
- **User types:** remove `balance` from `User`, `UserResponse`, and `UserLoginResponse`. Define separate account types.
- **User repository:** remove `balance` from `mapToUser`.
- **Auth service:** remove `createdUser.balance` and `user.balance` from registration/login results.
- **Account functionality:** add a repository, service, controller, and routes for creating and listing accounts. Use the authenticated user’s ID to establish ownership and verify access.
- **Registration behavior:** decide whether registration automatically opens an account. If so, create the user and account in one database transaction.
- **Existing data:** migrate any existing user balances into account rows before dropping the old balance column.

Two details also matter:

- Your SQL uses `scammistanbank`, while the API currently defaults to `ScammistanBank`, including a hardcoded health check. Use consistent casing—database names can be case-sensitive on your Linux VPS. [MariaDB case sensitivity](https://mariadb.com/docs/server/reference/sql-structure/sql-language-structure/identifier-case-sensitivity)
- The sample password placeholder cannot authenticate. Register through the API to generate a bcrypt hash; inserting a user also won’t automatically create an account.

